from author_proof_obligations import add
add('P133-0001','omitted formal verification','§1.8: functoriality of inductive limits','A chosen colimit sends a morphism of diagrams to a unique morphism of colimits; identity and composition are respected.','Apply the universal property to the compatible composites into the target colimit; compare composites on every structure map. Distinguish objectwise existence from a set-theoretically permissible global choice of representatives.','inductive limit|colimit|functoriality')
add('P134-0001','proof partly supplied, remainder omitted','Proposition 1.8: existence and right exactness of colimits','In an abelian category with small coproducts, a small inductive system has its colimit as the quotient of the coproduct by the transition relations; colimit is right exact.','Check that the displayed relation subobject imposes exactly the cocone identities, then use the adjunction between colimit and the constant-diagram functor to show preservation of cokernels and finite sums. The object and morphism indices must be sets.','colimit|right exact|coproduct')
add('P134-0002','proof supplied with scope qualification','Proposition 1.9.1: generators and coproduct presentations','In the stated abelian setting, the generator condition is equivalent to every object being a quotient of a coproduct of copies of the generator.','The printed proof gives the evaluation epimorphism. Its final remark about not using additivity must not erase the use of images and the passage from proper subobjects to detection of distinct maps. The separate general-category well-poweredness assertion requires the pullback qualification recorded in the check events.','generator|well-powered|evaluation','TOH-P134-S1.9-generator-wellpowered')
add('P135-0001','incorrect asserted immediate verification','Proposition 1.9.2: generators for diagram categories','The supported-at-one-vertex object does not generally satisfy the claimed evaluation adjunction in a diagram category with arrows.','Use the actual left adjoint to evaluation: at vertex t take the coproduct of A over paths s to t modulo the specified relations, or over morphisms in the indexing category. Then prove the evaluation adjunction and combine generators. The recorded two-vertex example refutes the printed shortcut.','diagram category|generator|evaluation adjoint','TOH-P135-P1.9.2-generators')
add('P135-0002','claim left without proof','§1.9: existence of a cogenerator','An AB5 abelian category with a generator has a cogenerator; Q/Z is a cogenerator of abelian groups.','For the general assertion, combine enough injectives with a set of quotients of a generator and injective envelopes or a product of suitable injectives, and verify that each nonzero object admits a nonzero map to the result. For abelian groups, extend a nonzero character of a cyclic subgroup to Q/Z using divisibility and injectivity. A mere existence of arbitrary injectives does not by itself supply one cogenerator without the set argument.','cogenerator|injective cogenerator|Q/Z')
add('P135-0003','definition-level verification','§1.10: injective effacement','The identity of M is an injective effacement exactly when M is injective; a monomorphism into an injective object is an injective effacement.','Unpack the lifting diagram in the definition; extend the prescribed map from the subobject using injectivity. This is a short formal implication rather than a new existence theorem.','injective effacement|injective object')
add('P135-0004','omitted formal verification','§1.9, footnote 2: posets as diagram shapes','A strictly ordered set is represented by arrows for strict inequalities and transitivity relations, with identity arrows separately understood.','Identify composable strict paths with the single arrow for their endpoints and check that all relations involving identities are automatic. Antisymmetry matters to the asserted absence of nontrivial paths returning to a vertex.','ordered set|diagram category|relations')
add('P136-0001','proof supplied','Theorem 1.10.1, Lemma 1: generator form of the injectivity criterion','Extension from all subobjects of a generator implies injectivity under the stated AB5 hypotheses.','The printed Zorn argument supplies the proof. Its chain upper bound uses AB5 for the union, and the extension over B+j(U) uses the displayed pushout presentation with the minus sign. This note records the dependencies, not an omitted theorem or a newly certified whole proof.','Baer criterion|AB5|injectivity')
add('P136-0002','construction with omitted verifications','Theorem 1.10.1: first effacement stage and transfinite construction','The map A to M_1(A) is monic, all maps from subobjects of U extend at the next stage, and the construction iterates functorially.','Prove monicity of the direct sum of the subobject inclusions by AB4, then use the pushout to prove A embeds. Specify M_0=A, successor maps, and AB5 colimits at limit ordinals. The later stabilization argument requires an ordinal of cofinality exceeding the cardinal of the set of subobjects of U.','injective embedding|transfinite|AB5')
add('P137-0001','dual statements left to reader','Theorem 1.10.1, Remark 4: projective duals','The dual assertions concern projective objects and projective effacements.','Reverse arrows and replace coproducts, generators and filtered unions by products, cogenerators and the corresponding inverse-limit axioms. Do not transfer the conclusion to a Grothendieck category without these dual hypotheses.','projective object|cogenerator|dual axioms')
add('P138-0001','omitted equivalence verification','§1.11: complete subcategories','The five-term closure condition is equivalent to closure under kernels and cokernels of internal maps and extensions; such a full subcategory is abelian with the inherited exact structure.','Extract kernels and cokernels by taking zero endpoints. Conversely break a five-term exact sequence into images and use internal kernels, cokernels and extension closure. Verify that zero and finite biproducts are included and that coimage to image remains an isomorphism. Modern terminology is weak Serre subcategory.','complete subcategory|weak Serre|kernel cokernel')
add('P138-0002','omitted equivalence verification','§1.11: thick subcategories','The three-term closure condition is equivalent to closure under subobjects, quotients and extensions (with a zero object).','Use degenerate short exact sequences to obtain the individual closure properties, then factor a three-term exact sequence through its image and express the middle object as an extension. Nonemptiness or inclusion of zero must be understood. Modern terminology is Serre subcategory.','thick subcategory|Serre subcategory|extensions')
add('P139-0001','construction and proofs explicitly omitted','§1.11: the abelian quotient','For a Serre subcategory, the roof construction gives an abelian quotient with exact projection and kernel equal to that subcategory.','Prove directedness/cofinality of the chosen subobjects and quotients, independence of representatives, associativity, and identities; construct kernels and cokernels of roofs and show the induced coimage-image map is invertible. Check the Hom colimits are sets in the universe used. Exactness then transports finite-page spectral sequence operations; convergence needs the filtration hypotheses separately.','Serre quotient|localization|spectral sequence')
add('P140-0001','definition-level verification','§2.1: morphisms of boundary functors','Degreewise addition and composition preserve naturality and compatibility with connecting morphisms.','Write the two commutative boundary squares and use bilinearity and composition. No additional existence result is being asserted.','delta functor|connecting morphism')
add('P140-0002','omitted universal-property verification','§2.1: uniqueness and truncation of satellites','A universal extension of F is unique up to its canonical comparison isomorphism; the satellite in a fixed degree is compatible with the allowed truncations.','Apply universality in both directions to uniqueness, and prove that restricting the chosen universal extension retains the needed universal property through the specified degree. Respect the corrected degree ranges and variances; uniqueness alone is not a proof of existence.','satellite|universal delta functor|truncation')
add('P141-0001','iteration asserted without proof','§2.1: iteration of satellites','Positive and negative satellites can be constructed successively from the corresponding first satellites.','Construct the connecting maps after each iteration, prove their naturality and the universal mapping property by induction, and check that the two variance conventions agree with the corrected signs. The bare objectwise identities do not establish a compatible full boundary functor.','satellite|dimension shifting|universal delta functor')
add('P141-0002','proof supplied, comparison details suppressed','Proposition 2.2.1: effaceability gives universality','Effaceability in positive degrees determines the extension of a natural transformation uniquely.','The displayed cokernel argument constructs degree one. Compare two effacements via their direct sum or a pushout, prove naturality, and iterate for higher degrees; treat the converse by embedding into an injective when that hypothesis is available.','effaceable|universal delta functor|dimension shifting')
add('P142-0001','criterion stated, proof deferred','Theorem 2.2.2: exactness criterion for satellites','The semiexactness and three-subobject condition characterize exactness in the stated setting; enough injectives make the additional condition automatic.','Prove the long exact sequence across the degree-zero joins, track the maps for P contained in Q contained in R, and use a common injective embedding to show the obstruction vanishes when enough injectives exist. The footnote refers to an external proof rather than supplying one here.','satellite|semiexact|injective effacement')
add('P142-0002','proof explicitly deferred','Theorem 2.2.2: existence, universality and duality','Cokernels obtained from injective effacements define positive satellites; the dual construction defines negative satellites, coeffaceable in strictly negative degrees.','Construct comparison maps between effacements and prove independence, connecting-map axioms, universality, and the exactness criterion. The degree-zero functor is not generally coeffaceable: use the corrected strict negative range and distinguish the stitched exact boundary functor from an unjustified universal claim in all degrees.','satellite|coeffaceable|negative degree','TOH-P142-dual-coeffaceability')
add('P142-0003','unproved construction and explicit open comparison','§2.2: satellites for an essentially small source','With a small skeleton and sufficient colimits in the target, satellites are constructed by a set-indexed universal process; AB5 supplies exactness under the stated criterion. The two Ext constructions must still be compared.','Give the indexing category of presentations or extensions, show the proposed colimit has the universal property, and use filtered exactness at the exactness step. To identify the two Ext constructions, compare both with Yoneda extensions and check boundary maps and products. Treat the algebraic-group example with its stated characteristic-zero and properness hypotheses.','small skeleton|satellite|Yoneda Ext')
add('P144-0001','comparison details omitted','§2.3: partial derived bifunctors and balance','Resolving the second variable gives a cohomological functor in the first if the bifunctor is exact there on injective second arguments; projective-injective balance identifies the two derived constructions.','Use a comparison map between injective resolutions and its homotopy to prove independence of the long exact boundary maps, including naturality in both variables. In the balanced case take the double complex of a projective resolution and an injective resolution and compare its two filtrations with the chosen sign convention.','derived bifunctor|balanced|Ext')
add('P144-0002','interpretation left without proof','§2.3: Ext first degree classifies extensions','Ext^1(A,B) identifies with equivalence classes of short exact sequences with quotient A and subobject B, and the group law agrees with Baer sum.','Push an extension into an injective embedding B to I and identify its class with the connecting cokernel; build the inverse using a pullback of I to I/B. Verify equivalence of extensions, naturality, and the Baer-sum group law. No enough-projectives hypothesis is required.','Ext1|Yoneda extension|Baer sum')
add('P145-0001','external theory explicitly assumed','§2.4: spectral sequence background','The spectral-sequence machinery is referred to Cartan–Eilenberg rather than proved in this paper.','Specify exact couples or filtered complexes, the induced differentials and convergence convention, then verify the boundedness assumptions of each use below. This note records a background dependency rather than asserting that all of that theory was reproved here.','spectral sequence|filtered complex|convergence')
add('P146-0001','construction referred to background','§2.4, equation (2.4.2): hypercohomology spectral sequences','For a bounded-below complex the two spectral sequences have corrected terms H^p(R^q F(K)) and R^p F(H^q K), with the common derived abutment.','Construct an appropriate Cartan–Eilenberg resolution, totalize with the signed differential, and identify both E2 pages; boundedness supplies finite diagonals in each total degree and the stated convergence. Naturality needs comparison maps, not merely equality of the displayed E2 expressions.','hypercohomology|Cartan Eilenberg|spectral sequence')
add('P146-0002','injective-complex characterization asserted','§2.4: injectives in the category of nonnegative complexes','The stated split complexes with injective terms and vanishing positive cohomology are injective objects of the complex category; every nonnegative complex embeds in one.','Decompose such complexes into an injective object in degree zero and contractible two-term injective disks, establish the associated Hom lifting property, and construct an embedding degree by degree. Use finite products in each degree and check that the differential and degree-zero kernel fit the decomposition.','injective complex|contractible|disk complex')
add('P147-0001','proof sketch plus unproved unbounded extension','§2.4: resolution comparison and unbounded finite-dimension case','The bounded-below constructions agree through comparison of double complexes; the extension to unbounded complexes under finite injective dimension is stated without proof.','For the bounded case give compatible comparison homotopies and identify the finite-diagonal filtrations. For the unbounded assertion specify the totalization and derived-complex framework, use finite cohomological dimension to control truncation and convergence, and verify independence. The short assertion is not by itself a general license to use arbitrary unbounded double-complex spectral sequences.','unbounded derived category|finite cohomological dimension|K-injective')
add('P148-0001','construction sketched','Theorem 2.4.1: spectral sequence of a composite','If F is left exact and sends injectives to G-acyclic objects, with G left exact, the spectral sequence R^p G(R^q F(A)) abuts to R^(p+q)(GF)(A).','Use an injective resolution of A and a Cartan–Eilenberg resolution of its image under F; identify the first filtration with the derived composite using the acyclicity hypothesis and the second with the displayed E2 page. Check naturality, edge maps and first-quadrant convergence.','Grothendieck spectral sequence|composite functor|acyclic')
add('P148-0002','equivalence asserted','Theorem 2.4.1, Remark 1: effaceable composite obstruction','The acyclicity-on-injectives condition is equivalent to effaceability of the positive (R^qG)F in the stated setting.','If effaceable, apply an effacement to an injective object; its splitting forces the functor value to vanish. Conversely embed every object in an injective object, which simultaneously kills every positive degree. This also explains why separate effaceability yields the joint acyclic embedding required in the text.','effaceable|acyclic injectives|composite functor')
add('P148-0003','comparison asserted','Theorem 2.4.1, Remark 2: acyclic resolutions','An F-acyclic resolution computes the composite spectral sequence through hypercohomology of its F-image.','Compare it to an injective resolution, prove the induced map becomes a quasi-isomorphism after F by the acyclic-resolution lemma, and then compare the hypercohomology filtrations. Identify from which page the comparison is canonical and keep the boundedness assumptions.','acyclic resolution|hypercohomology|comparison')
add('P150-0001','naturality details suppressed','Proposition 2.5.2: canonical resolving-functor comparison','The comparison with the derived functor is independent of resolutions, natural, and compatible with connecting morphisms.','Use the horseshoe construction or a common resolution for a short exact sequence, prove compatibility of the connecting maps, and invoke universality with the prescribed degree-zero augmentation. This is the completion of the preceding proof, not a separate omitted existence theorem.','resolving functor|derived functor|comparison')
add('P150-0002','recursive construction verification omitted','§2.5: an exact resolution of the identity','An exact functor C^0 with a natural monomorphism from the identity yields an exact functorial resolution by iterated cokernels; if its terms are F-acyclic, applying F resolves F.','Induct using the snake lemma to show that the successive cokernel functors remain exact, define the differentials through the augmentations, and prove d squared is zero and the augmented complex exact. F-acyclicity gives termwise exactness after F and the acyclic-resolution comparison. Use the corrected H^n(F(C(A))) expression.','resolution of identity|Godement resolution|exact functor')
